-0.01055145263671875, 0.047149658203125, 0.01300811767578125, 0.0198822021484375, 0.006572723388671875, 0.0110015869140625, 0.01065826416015625, 0.01031494140625, -0.045440673828125, -0.006591796875, 0.0013093948364257812, -0.002178192138671875, 0.044769287109375, -0.031951904296875, 0.005619049072265625, -0.016754150390625, -0.0027618408203125, -0.00128936767578125, -0.00946807861328125, 0.01096343994140625, 0.01593017578125, 0.00652313232421875, 0.0204620361328125, -0.006610870361328125, 0.02960205078125, -0.0120391845703125, 0.01462554931640625, -0.00824737548828125, 0.0176239013671875, 0.006511688232421875, -0.01457977294921875, -0.01239013671875, -0.00888824462890625, -0.0214080810546875, -0.0212860107421875, -0.00501251220703125, 0.0191802978515625, -0.048553466796875, 0.006412506103515625, 0.00844573974609375, 0.011199951171875, -0.0052947998046875, -0.0133209228515625, -0.0239715576171875, 0.0091552734375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. In level-k models, a \"level-0\" player is typically defined as one who does not engage in strategic reasoning about others' actions and instead follows a simple, non-strategic rule\u2014such as choosing randomly, selecting a salient or focal option, or following a default heuristic. However, there is ongoing debate about the appropriate definition and operationalization of level-0 behavior. Some models assume uniform randomization (e.g., Stahl and Wilson, 1995), while others propose that level-0 choices are guided by salience, focal points, or context-dependent heuristics (e.g., Crawford et al., 2013; Wright and Leyton-Brown, 2014). Our study adopts an explicit, context-sensitive approach, operationalizing level-0 reasoning as either the action participants believe a non-strategic player would take or the action they themselves would take when instructed to play as a level-0 player, with explicit instructions to ignore strategic considerations. We acknowledge that this approach is one of several in the literature and that alternative operationalizations may yield different results.\n\nTo clarify terminology, we define \"level-0 action\" as the choice a participant (or group) makes when instructed to act as a level-0 player, explicitly told to disregard strategic reasoning and focus only on what seems natural, obvious, or salient. \"Level-0 belief,\" in contrast, refers to the participant's (or group's) belief about what a generic, non-strategic player would do in the same situation. In practice, these constructs were elicited in separate tasks: for the action, groups were instructed to make a choice as if they themselves were the level-0 player; for the belief, they were asked to predict what a typical level-0 player would do, again with instructions to ignore strategic reasoning. Instructions were delivered in writing and read aloud by the experimenter to ensure clarity and consistency. The distinction was reinforced by requiring groups to complete each elicitation in a separate discussion period, with the order of tasks counterbalanced across sessions.\n\nThis study addresses two main research questions: (1) To what extent do level-0 actions and beliefs align in strategic games, and (2) How does this alignment vary across different game contexts? Here, \"alignment\" refers to the degree of correspondence between the action participants select when instructed to play as level-0 and their belief about what a level-0 player would do, measured by the similarity of group consensus responses for each. \"Salience\" is defined as the perceived naturalness, obviousness, or focality of an option, as reflected in group discussions and choice patterns. Our primary hypothesis is that this correspondence depends on the structure and salient features of the game, and may diverge in settings where standard level-k predictions fail.\n\nWe employ the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), which involves group discussion and consensus. This method is chosen over individual elicitation to encourage explicit reasoning and to capture the shared understanding of level-0 behavior, which may be more robust than isolated individual intuitions. We recognize, however, that group consensus can introduce biases such as conformity pressure or dominance by outspoken members, potentially masking individual differences. To mitigate these effects, groups were instructed both in writing and verbally to ensure all voices were heard, and facilitators were trained to monitor for overt dominance or conformity. Facilitators intervened if any participant was not contributing or if one member dominated the discussion, though we did not systematically record the frequency or effectiveness of such interventions. We acknowledge that the absence of individual-level responses is a limitation and that our findings reflect group-level consensus rather than within-group heterogeneity.\n\nParticipants (N = 120, mean age = 21.4, 54% female) were randomly assigned to 40 groups of three using a computerized randomization procedure upon arrival. All sessions were conducted in a laboratory setting. Upon arrival, participants were randomly assigned to groups and seated at separate tables to minimize cross-group communication. The experimental protocol consisted of two games: the canonical beauty contest and a variant of the 11-20 game (Goeree et al., 2018). For each game, groups first received written instructions defining a level-0 player as \"someone who does not think strategically about others and simply chooses an option based on what seems natural, obvious, or salient, without considering payoffs or others' choices.\" Instructions were also read aloud by the experimenter to ensure comprehension. Groups were then asked to discuss and reach consensus on (a) what action a level-0 player would take (\"level-0 action\") and (b) what they believe a level-0 player would do (\"level-0 belief\"), with the order of these tasks counterbalanced across sessions using a Latin square design. Each discussion was limited to 10 minutes and audio-recorded for later qualitative analysis. After each discussion, groups submitted their consensus response via computer interface. Experimenters monitored for experimenter demand effects by minimizing interaction with participants during discussions and by standardizing all instructions.\n\nData were analyzed using descriptive statistics, paired t-tests, and effect size calculations (Cohen's d). To account for group clustering, we also estimated mixed-effects models with random intercepts for groups, which yielded qualitatively similar results to the t-tests. For the beauty contest game, both elicited level-0 beliefs and observed level-0 actions clustered tightly around 50 (beliefs: M = 49.8, SD = 3.2; actions: M = 50.1, SD = 2.9), with no significant difference (t(39) = 0.42, p = 0.68, 95% CI [-1.6, 2.2], Cohen's d = 0.07). In the 11-20 game, elicited level-0 beliefs were more dispersed (M = 14.7, SD = 2.6), while level-0 actions were highly concentrated on 11 (M = 11.8, SD = 1.1), with a significant difference (t(39) = 6.13, p < 0.001, 95% CI [2.0, 3.8], Cohen's d = 0.97). These results were robust to nonparametric Wilcoxon signed-rank tests and to the inclusion of group as a random effect in mixed models.\n\nQualitative analysis of group discussions was conducted using thematic coding. Two independent coders reviewed all transcripts, identifying references to \"salience,\" \"naturalness,\" and \"focal points\" as guiding principles for level-0 choices. Inter-rater reliability was high (Cohen's kappa = 0.82). Illustrative quotes include: \"11 just stands out as the lowest, so it's the obvious pick if you're not thinking about others,\" and \"50 is right in the middle, so it feels natural for someone not strategizing.\" Both patterns deviate from the standard assumption that level-0 players randomize uniformly.\n\nOur findings indicate that the alignment between level-0 beliefs and actions is context-dependent. In the beauty contest, alignment is high and consistent with prior studies (e.g., Nagel, 1995; Burchardi and Penczynski, 2014), supporting the midpoint heuristic. In the 11-20 game, alignment is low, and both beliefs and actions deviate from uniform randomization, consistent with Goeree et al. (2018). However, given our focus on only two games and a single university subject pool, these results should be interpreted cautiously and may not generalize to other populations or game structures.\n\nThe divergence between beliefs and actions in the 11-20 game appears to be driven by the salience of the lowest number as a focal point, as indicated by group discussions and the concentration of choices on 11. However, our evidence for context-dependent heuristics is correlational, not causal. Future research should experimentally manipulate salience (e.g., by highlighting or obscuring certain options) or vary payoff structures to test causal mechanisms.\n\nLimitations of our study include the use of laboratory settings, a consensus-based group method that may obscure individual differences, and a narrow set of games. Group dynamics such as conformity, dominant voices, and experimenter demand effects may have influenced results, and while facilitators were trained to mitigate these, we cannot rule out residual effects. We did not collect individual-level responses, so we cannot assess within-group heterogeneity. Additional potential confounds include cultural background, prior exposure to similar games, and interpretation of instructions. The limited number of games and homogeneity of the sample further restrict generalizability. Future research should address these limitations by collecting both individual and group responses, recruiting more diverse samples, and systematically varying game features and focal points to better understand the foundations of level-0 reasoning.\n\nIn conclusion, our findings suggest that models of strategic reasoning should incorporate more flexible, context-sensitive specifications of level-0 behavior, rather than relying on uniform randomization or fixed heuristics. Modelers should consider allowing level-0 behavior to depend on salient features of the game and the payoff structure. However, given the limited scope of our study, further research is needed to establish the generality and causal mechanisms underlying context-dependent level-0 reasoning."}}